{{$NEXT}}
  - Breaking: the parser recognises headings by CommonMark's rules. Fenced
    code blocks of ``` and ~~~ (closer of the same character and at least as
    long, no backtick in a backtick fence's info string), a leading YAML
    front matter block, HTML comment blocks and lines indented by four or
    more spaces no longer yield headings, so the addresses of the sections
    below them change; ATX headings indented by one to three spaces are now
    headings. Setext headings stay unsupported. update_paragraph no longer
    cuts a closing fence or front matter delimiter off a page.
  - Breaking: get_toc anchors follow GitHub: letters and digits of every
    script are kept ("Über Größe" is "über-größe", it was "ber-gr-e"; "日本語"
    is no longer empty), other punctuation is dropped, each space becomes a
    hyphen ("A & B" is "a--b", it was "a-b"), and equal anchors on a page
    get -1, -2, ... The section length and preview still count the heading
    line.
  - Breaking: headings that share a heading_path are all reachable. The
    second and later one gets a suffix on its last segment ("Page#Notes[2]",
    "[3]", ...) in get_toc and in every tool, history tools included; the
    first keeps its address. Before, the later ones were silently
    unreachable and update_paragraph always edited the first.
  - A page that starts with a UTF-8 byte order mark no longer loses its
    first heading: the mark is ignored when parsing and stays in the file
    when a section is written. Pages with a mark now get the headings'
    real addresses ("Title#Sub", not "Sub").
  - Breaking: a closing run of # ends a heading's text only after whitespace,
    so "# C#" is "C#" (it was "C"). In a heading_path, "\" and "#" inside a
    heading's text are written "\\" and "\#", and a text ending in "[digits]"
    has that "[" written "\[": the heading "A#B" and the pair "A" > "B" no
    longer share an address. MCP::Wiki::TOC::Parser gets escape_segment and
    split_heading_path for the two directions.
  - list_pages and get_toc can be asked for less: list_pages takes an optional
    prefix, a directory, and lists and walks only that one; get_toc takes
    max_level (1-6) to leave out deeper headings and include_preview (false
    drops content_preview). Without them the answers are as before, and no
    heading_path changes
  - commit_reason_required (--commit-reason-required) now works: every tool
    that writes refuses a call without a non-empty reason and says so in its
    description. rename_page and delete_page take an optional reason for
    this (also used as the git commit message and passed to on_change
    handlers when given)
  - With use_git, restore_section is committed like every other write, with
    its reason as message, else "Restore <heading_path> from <short hash>".
    The restored text used to be committed later with an unrelated edit of
    the page, under that edit's message
  - Breaking: rename_page and delete_page refuse a page name that is itself a
    symlink (also as the target of a rename). They used to move or remove the
    page the link points to and leave the link dangling; name that page
    instead. Reading and writing through a link work as before
  - create_page without content starts the page with the file name without
    ".md" as its heading ("# c" for a/b/c.md), no longer the whole path;
    directories that a rename or delete empties stay
  - Docker: one image, raudssus/mcp-wiki on Docker Hub, built and pushed by
    the dzil bundle at release; the second, separate build-and-push script
    and the local-only registry setting are gone
  - Docker: the entrypoint starts mcp-wiki without a shell in between, so a
    wiki path or any argument containing spaces or quotes arrives intact; it
    runs as the owner of the mounted wiki
  - Docker: the image no longer pins PERL5LIB to one perl version, and
    building it no longer tries to build an image from inside the image
  - bin/mcp-wiki starts with "#!/usr/bin/env perl"; installs rewrite it to the
    installing perl as before
  - restore_section writes the restored page: the document that the history
    returns replaces the page file instead of being pushed into a single
    section, which used to leave the whole page inside one heading.
  - Breaking: a section's text is its body without the heading line, in
    get_paragraph and update_paragraph alike. update_paragraph replaces the
    body only and leaves the heading and the following section untouched
    (it used to swallow the first line of the next section); content that
    still starts with the heading line now gets that heading twice. Writing
    back what get_paragraph returned leaves the page byte for byte as it was.
  - Breaking: heading_path is now the chain of a section's ancestors only.
    Siblings no longer leak into it: "# Intro", "## Alpha", "## Beta" yield
    Intro#Beta (was Intro#Alpha#Beta), and a following "# Other" yields
    Other (was Intro#Other). A section's address no longer changes when a
    sibling section is inserted before it. Addresses stored from earlier
    versions for any section after a sibling must be fetched anew via get_toc.
  - MCP::Wiki::Git stays as a deprecated thin wrapper around
    MCP::Wiki::Git::History
  - Removed unused internals: Document update_content_hash, Git::History
    history_file / _build_history_file (.mcp-wiki-history.json was never read
    or written), and the unused Digest::SHA, to_json and Types::Standard
    imports; Digest::SHA and Types::Standard leave the cpanfile
  - Docs: README tool table lists the real arguments of every tool
    (heading_path, not heading); mcp-wiki POD states what --git and
    --commit-reason-required do; PLAN.md is marked as the original draft;
    t/06-readme-tools.t keeps README and bin/mcp-wiki POD in sync with the
    tool schemas and Getopt options
  - Security: every tool is confined to the wiki root given at start
    (wiki_root / --wiki-root). Tools take no root_dir argument; page names are
    resolved (symlinks, "..") and must lie below the root.
  - Security: a page is a .md file. Tools refuse any other file and everything
    below a .git directory, so a wiki's repository is out of reach.
  - list_pages returns exactly the pages the other tools accept and does not
    enter .git
  - create_page and rename_page create missing subdirectories
  - cpanfile matches the code: MCP, Mojolicious, Moo, Path::Tiny and
    JSON::MaybeXS are required, Git::Raw is recommended (history degrades
    cleanly without it), unused Markdown::Perl and Syntax::Keyword::Try are
    dropped; t/05-cpanfile.t keeps it in sync
  - Docs: the README shows the Docker usage as "docker run -i --rm -v
    /path/to/wiki:/wiki raudssus/mcp-wiki"; the Document SYNOPSIS uses
    file_path; commit_reason_required is documented as having no effect; a
    TOC entry's content_preview and char_count are documented as including
    the heading line
  - The distribution's own test suite no longer fails during cpanm, dzil test
    or the Docker build: a Docker test read README.md, which the tarball
    does not ship
  - get_toc and the section tools no longer slow down quadratically with the
    number of headings (8000 headings: about 42 s down to under 1 s); heading
    paths and line ranges are unchanged
  - A tool call that fails reading or deleting a page, or in the git history,
    now answers with an error naming the problem instead of an opaque
    "Internal error" (e.g. an unreadable page, a directory named like a
    page, a failing history lookup or restore)
  - namespace::clean is declared as a runtime requirement: MCP::Wiki::Git
    loads it, so a clean install failed t/00-load and t/41
  - get_paragraph and update_paragraph read the page once and work on that
    snapshot; a write landing in between can no longer make an edit hit the
    wrong section
  - Pages with CRLF line endings: get_paragraph returns the section with LF
    line endings, update_paragraph writes the page back with CRLF throughout,
    and an unchanged round trip stays byte-identical; TOC previews and char
    counts no longer count the CRs, and an empty "# " heading line no longer
    yields a heading named by a stray CR. Pages with LF line endings are
    unchanged.
  - With --git, creating and updating a page now commits it, authored with the
    repository's git identity or "MCP::Wiki <mcp-wiki@localhost>" when none
    is configured; get_section_history and restore_section now work,
    including non-ASCII headings and text. Before, every commit failed and
    the history tools could not read the repository. JSON::MaybeXS is no
    longer a runtime requirement.
  - update_paragraph and restore_section refuse pages that are not valid UTF-8
    instead of replacing every non-UTF-8 byte with U+FFFD; the file is left
    untouched, and reading such a page still works.
  - restore_section no longer drops the page's final newline or the blank line
    before the next heading, and a page restored from a version with CRLF
    line endings no longer gets stray carriage returns.
  - get_section_history lists only the commits that created or changed the
    section; commits touching other pages or other sections are left out.
  - Declare perl 5.020 as the minimum version, the floor MCP and
    JSON::Schema::Tiny already require, and keep the signatures feature from
    warning on perl 5.20 to 5.34.
  - update_paragraph and restore_section keep the page's file permissions
    instead of widening them (a 0600 page no longer becomes 0644), and refuse
    a page the server may not write instead of replacing it.
  - With --git, renaming and deleting a page are now committed (the old name
    removed, the new one added) instead of failing silently and leaving the
    repository out of step with the files.
  - With --git, a given reason is the commit message as is (no repeated
    "Reason:" trailer); without one the generated message is used.
    restore_section's reason is described as what it is: passed on in the
    restore event, not committed.
  - on_change events name pages by their canonical path below the wiki root
    (as list_pages shows them), also when the client used ./, .. or an
    in-root symlink, so auto-commit stages the right file; each handler gets
    its own copy of the event. bin/mcp-wiki no longer installs a debug
    handler that warned on every change.
  - Pages and directories with non-ASCII names work in every tool: list_pages
    returns them as text instead of double-encoded, pages below a non-ASCII
    directory are found again, error messages name them correctly, and names
    that are not valid UTF-8 are skipped with a warning.
  - list_pages skips a directory it cannot read (such as a root-only
    lost+found) with a warning instead of failing.
  - A directory, FIFO or device named like a page is refused by every tool;
    rename_page no longer moves such a directory.
  - Tool errors no longer show absolute paths or Perl source locations; an
    unknown heading_path is answered with the page's heading paths (up to
    50).
  - create_page declares its optional reason; heading_path is described as
    the full path from get_toc with an example; create_page and get_toc say
    how they behave; every tool carries MCP annotations (read-only,
    destructive, idempotent hints); serverInfo reports the distribution's
    version instead of 1.0.0.
  - create_page and rename_page no longer replace a page that appeared
    between their existence check and the write.
  - get_section_history and restore_section find a page's history whatever
    spelling the client uses (./a.md, sub/../a.md, an in-root symlink).
  - With --git, a rename without reason is committed as "Renamed page: old ->
    new" instead of "Renamed page: ".
  - The POD documents every public method and lists the tools.

0.001  2026-04-28
  - Initial release
  - MCP server for markdown wiki management
  - TOC extraction and paragraph-level editing
  - Git integration for history tracking